<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # This Is My Amazing Community. – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Amazing Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is My Amazing Community.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Infer meaning from context clues in short texts.
reading_2: Interpret simple charts and graphs related to texts.
phonemic_awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can infer meaning from context clues about community events by identifying time expressions (e.g., 'Tomorrow we will have a community fair.' 'The fair was held last month.').
Can interpret data in charts about community engagement (e.g., 'Ten students like to go to the movies.').
Can demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words (e.g., recognizing /ʊ/ in 'cook' and /uː/ in 'school').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or images of different community places (e.g., a park, a market, a library, a school) and community roles (e.g., teacher, farmer, police officer). Students quickly identify the places and roles. The teacher then writes "Community Event" on the board and asks students to brainstorm words related to community events (e.g., 'festival', 'celebrate', 'enjoy', 'cook').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher presents a short, simple sentence like "The farmer cooks fresh food at the market every day." The teacher reads the sentence clearly, modeling pronunciation of the words. Then, the teacher highlights the words 'cook' and 'school' (from Lesson 1 if introduced) and explicitly models their short /ʊ/ and long /uː/ vowel sounds. Students repeat after the teacher. The teacher models how to quickly identify key words in a sentence by circling 'farmer', 'cooks', 'fresh food', 'market', 'every day'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the farmer cook food last month or every day?" (Checks understanding of 'every day' and present simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the food old or fresh?" (Checks understanding of 'fresh' through antonyms).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(For phonemic awareness) "Listen: /kʊk/. Is it 'cook' or 'cool'?" (Checks ability to distinguish short/long vowel sounds).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Amazing Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informational text (narrative excerpt from a community blog/newsletter) about a "Community Fun Day" with an accompanying simple bar graph titled "Favorite Activities at the Fun Day." The text includes vocabulary (community, park, festival, farmer, vendor, musician, delicious, traditional, gather, share, enjoy) and grammar (present simple, past simple with time expressions like 'last month', 'every year', present perfect 'has worked').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will be able to read and understand short, simple texts about community events and roles, inferring meaning from context and interpreting basic visual data.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Students read the title and first sentence. "What is this text about?" "Where does this event happen?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher presents 3-4 True/False statements about the text's main ideas. E.g., "The community has a festival every day." (False) "Many people enjoyed delicious food at the park." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match short phrases from the text to pictures or descriptions (e.g., 'traditional music' to a picture of a musician, 'fresh vegetables' to a picture of a farmer at a market).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Chart interpretation:</b> Students answer questions about the bar graph. E.g., "How many people like to visit the market?" "Which activity is the most popular?" "Which activity is the least popular?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice inferring meaning from context clues, identifying time expressions (Present Simple, Past Simple, Present Perfect), and interpreting data from charts. Phonemic awareness practice with short and long vowel sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Context Clues & Time Expressions (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a short text about a community event with certain words or phrases underlined. Students work individually to circle the context clues that help them understand the meaning of the underlined words (e.g., "The carpenter helped to <u>build</u> a new bench for the park. He used wood and tools." Students circle "used wood and tools".) The teacher also asks students to identify and highlight time expressions in the text (e.g., 'Last month', 'every year', 'now').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Vowel Sound Identification (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher writes a list of single-syllable words from the lesson's vocabulary on the board (e.g., 'cook', 'school', 'park', 'meet', 'job', 'sweet', 'build'). Students read each word aloud and then raise one finger for a short vowel sound or two fingers for a long vowel sound. The teacher then guides a class check, reiterating the sounds (/ʊ/ in 'cook', /uː/ in 'school').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Chart Interpretation & Inference (Pair Interaction):</b> Students receive a new short text about community responsibilities and a simple chart showing "Volunteer Hours Per Month." In pairs, students read the text and the chart. They then ask and answer questions about it, focusing on interpreting data and inferring meaning. E.g., Student A: "According to the chart, who volunteers the most?" Student B: "The gardener volunteers the most. What does 'gardener' do in our community, based on the text?" Student A: "A gardener helps keep the park beautiful." (Practices 'What does your neighbor do?' and 'How long has your teacher worked here?' if applicable).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Amazing Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will read an informational flyer for a "Neighborhood Clean-up and Fair" and interpret an accompanying simple pie chart showing "Reasons for Participating."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual reading and written response.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. The teacher distributes a flyer (short text) about an upcoming "Neighborhood Clean-up and Fair" event, including details about volunteers (e.g., "The community needs volunteers to share responsibilities like cleaning the park. We celebrated a successful fair last year!"), activities, time expressions ('Tomorrow we will have...', 'Last month we had...'), and some new vocabulary. An accompanying pie chart shows "Reasons for Participating" (e.g., 'To meet new friends', 'To help the community', 'To enjoy the festival').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Students read the flyer and analyze the pie chart individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Students then answer three comprehension questions in writing, demonstrating their reading skills:</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">a) "When is the Neighborhood Clean-up and Fair?" (Identifies time expression).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">b) "What does 'responsibilities' mean in the text?" (Infers meaning from context).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">c) "According to the chart, what is the most common reason people volunteer for the fair?" (Interprets chart).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. As an extension, students underline any word in the flyer containing a short /ʊ/ or long /uː/ sound and write it down.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist of student responses to the production task and quick review of written answers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' written answers from the Performance stage will be collected and reviewed. The teacher will check for accuracy in identifying time expressions, inferring meaning, and interpreting chart data, as well as correct identification of vowel sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to locate specific information and time expressions in the text (e.g., 'Tomorrow', 'Last year').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate inference of meaning from context clues (e.g., 'responsibilities').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct interpretation of data in the chart (e.g., identifying the most common reason).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated knowledge of short /ʊ/ and long /uː/ vowel sounds.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find dates and times in a text about my community: Yes / Maybe / Not yet."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand new words about my community by reading the sentences around them: Yes / Maybe / Not yet."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or idea about our community that I learned today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about our community was easy for me today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was challenging, and what can I do to improve my reading next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., specific activities, types of texts, student engagement).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support students' reading comprehension, especially with inferring meaning and interpreting visuals? (e.g., more visuals, simpler texts, pre-teaching more vocabulary).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How will this lesson connect to Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction)? The reading skills developed today (understanding community events, roles, and time expressions) will directly support students in discussing and asking questions about community activities and people in the next speaking-focused lesson.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>